WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Good morning John. It is the 11th annual Project for Awesome,

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:07.860
and that means that I'm here to tell you about why solving hard problems is so hard.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.240
There's a bit of a universal law in logistics that the last little bit of

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:18.269
transport that an item has to do to get to its destination is by far the most difficult leg of the journey.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.919
This is true in telecommunications. It's also true in, like, your circulatory system.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.550
It's called the last mile problem.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:32.160
Weirdly enough, it takes almost as much energy and work to get, like, something that I ordered off Amazon—say, this—

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.480
from like China where it's manufactured

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.840
all the way to, like, the distribution warehouse in Missoula, Montana

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.170
as it does to get it from the distribution warehouse in Missoula to my house.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.730
This is a very squeaky prop.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:45.680
So what's going on here?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.220
The moment this thing is manufactured in China it has like one place to go to.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:54.750
It goes to its port and then from there - from that port it can go to, like, maybe a dozen or so other ports in the world.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.099
Then when it arrives at whatever port gets to, it has maybe a few thousand different warehouses

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.260
or stores that could end up in.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.600
If it ends up in a store it has to wait for a

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:08.129
person to go there and buy it, but if it's being shipped directly to the person, directly to me

00:01:08.129 --> 00:01:12.100
then it ends up in this centralized local warehouse with tens of thousands of other products,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.340
and those tens of thousands of products have to

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.520
go to potentially hundreds of thousands of different individual places.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.380
For 99% of this thing's travels it could have gone to like one of a few thousand different places

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.600
and that last 1%,

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.660
it's gonna go to one of literally hundreds of millions of places.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.040
The last mile problem is kind of why oftentimes when we have really big ideas about how to fix things,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.360
those ideas don't work.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.400
Because when you get to the last mile your one overarching solution to all problems

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.180
becomes tens of thousands or hundreds of thousands of different solutions

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.280
that are all hard to work out.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:53.300
And that's all work, like hard work.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.780
And when it comes to delivering health care or water or food in parts of the world where those things aren't available,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.120
it used to be work that just didn't get done.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.040
But now people are doing it. Organizations like Save the Children and Last Mile Health.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.100
And they solve the last mile problem for health care the only way you can solve it:

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.217
with problem-solvers.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.760
They work with and train and employ people from those communities

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.980
to become community health care workers.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.440
Those people then help to grow the economy, to educate other people, and to keep their communities,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.040
and thus the entire global community, more healthy.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.040
So this costs money.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.840
But let me venture that maybe this is one of the best things

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.880
we could possibly be spending money on as a society.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.340
But what if in addition to helping fund that very cool work of solving that very hard problem,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.880
you also get cool stuff!

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.200
Well, that's why you should go to projectforawesome.com/donate

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.540
where for the first part of the Project For Awesome, all of the money

